[Music]  kovid 19 has ripped through  india leaving horror and heartbreak in  its wake  officially the virus has killed 400 000  indians but the real death toll is  thought to be much higher  the race is on to vaccinate the country  though not everyone is convinced  the government needs to tell us why we  should get it what are the benefits  they are failing to give us information  it's especially a problem in rural areas  where two-thirds of indians live  vaccine hesitancy is a major threat to  india's race  to beat the pandemic  [Music]  india's devastating second wave of  coronavirus infections appears to be  ebbing away  the focus now is on vaccinating as many  people as possible  to prevent a third one however in a  country as diverse as  india there remains a diversity of  opinion towards vaccinations  namisha jaiswal reports from the north  of the country  it's their turn to get vaccinated  and these young residents of rohitak in  the north indian state of haryana  have been eagerly waiting for it after a  month of low vaccine availability  supply is finally catching up with the  demand here  officials say online slots in the small  city are booked quickly  and allowing offline registrations in  june has improved access to the jab even  further  doctors believe india's massive second  wave  could have erased vaccine hesitancy  after the second wave people are  scrambling to get vaccinated  they say please vaccinate us we  definitely want protection from this  virus  i believe vaccine hesitancy is  negligible  in a village 20 kilometers from the  hospital  these men paint a very different picture  only two of them have been vaccinated  because they volunteered in the vaccine  trials  and even they don't feel confident about  the benefits of the vaccine  in the last two months more than 50  people have died in this village  these men say it wasn't the corona virus  that killed them  but fear even doctors have died after  vaccinations  there was a vaccine they recalled i  think now they are saying plasma therapy  isn't useful  they just keep changing their stance  people aren't afraid the air is clean  here the problem is in the cities  because of the air  they're felling trees it's a city  problem it is in the villages  the village's local health center wears  a deserted look  all year only 600 people have been  vaccinated  supplies are limited and there isn't a  rush anywhere  and it's all the more reason that the  few who do show up  stand out sunil bhardwaj traveled here  from a nearby village to get his father  the second dose  many people worry about fevers after the  vaccine my father had one  too but i got it as well and i had no  side effects at all  the vaccine is very important everyone  must get it  bhardwaj may be a lone voice here right  now  but he's emphatic he tells everyone  around him  that the vaccine can save lives  india is slowly solving its supply  crisis  but it may face an uphill battle against  skepticism  to prevent a crisis of demand as well  well let's speak to anway shaduta who is  a senior researcher at the christian  mickelson  institute thanks for joining us on the  covid 19 special can you just explain to  us  how governments in india are trying to  tackle the challenge of vaccine  hesitancy  thanks rob for that question and thank  you for having me on the show  but as we know vaccine hesitance in  itself is a very complex phenomenon and  we even see the whole anti-vaxxer  movement  in the global north where people are  much more educated so clearly education  is not a reason that makes people take  vaccination or not  now coming to india we see more  hesitancy in rural areas  as opposed to urban areas and there are  several causes to that  and very briefly let me touch upon those  so first being in rural areas  historically there has been an  aversion towards western allopathic  medication  and as opposed to urban areas where  people have had more access to health  care to begin with and that's allopathic  health care  now add to this makes other factors like  religion  caste gender so for example there's been  a lot of hesitation amongst the muslim  community because they feel that there  is big fat in the vaccination or the  jain community in  india who think that the vaccination is  not vegetarian or the hindu community  who might think there is cow serum in  the vaccination  we also see that the vaccination rates  are higher amongst  men and then women given that it's a  patriarchal society  as well and finally added to that  are various other factors like mistrust  in the state  itself and also if we see  india has been very successful in its  polio vaccination program  but those are catered to children and  not necessarily adults  so all of this i think makes uh vaccine  hesitancy a very  complex problem and how is the indian  state dealing with it  so off late they have uh brought  together a lot of religious leaders  and they are making these religious  leaders appeal to their own communities  and talk to them  and to persuade them there have been  celebrities going on tv or local  politician  because there is also this neighborhood  effect so if someone in your locality  gets the vaccine  and you are more likely to take it so  those are the some of the  um uh uh yeah i mean the big issue is  diversity right i mean india is  an enormous country with enormous amount  of diversity you've talked about the  different things uh  you know in terms of you know city and  rural but also  religious groups how do you go about  tackling a pandemic in a in a  coordinated way when there's so much  difference  within the different areas  i think it has been a major challenge  and we can see the state kind of failed  especially um to contain the second wave  of the pandemic  and the part of that also rests in not  being prepared for what was coming  now how do you tackle it something that  really worked in india in some states as  opposed to other  so let's take kerala for example a state  that managed to curb this  um or contain or manage or govern the  pandemic very well  as opposed to one of the most populated  states in the world uttar pradesh  and what explains this are historical  investments  into decentralizing local governments  into building health care and other  infrastructures  which necessarily did not happen in case  of uttar pradesh due to say corruption  due to other political factors that have  been at play  and therefore is this about tailored  approaches to  two different areas is that what you  mean by the decentralization  yeah so decentralization means where you  decentralized  power to up to the local level and you  give say in indian case the panchayat  the local  governing bodies uh amount of powers  and resources to govern because they are  the last  leg of delivery of services to the  people in the  most remote rural areas and therefore  the management should have been  kind of top uh bottom up rather than you  know  complete top down and so we see a  diversity of responses and of course  within that we have to feature for  other social uh political uh stratifiers  as i mentioned before  is there anything that we've seen during  the second wave in india that leaves you  thinking  that a third wave can be prevented  so yeah a couple of things firstly i  think we have learned that we should not  already accept victory over a virus  which is still ongoing  we need to be more prepared and not  dismantle health care infrastructure and  other infrastructure facilities  and particularly we need to take into  account for example the migrant crisis  that affected the first wave  so that it could not have happened much  in the second wave so  taking into consideration the most  vulnerable groups and um  and so that in a third wave these  communities can be protected and there  can be more preparedness  anointing from the christian mickelson  institute thanks for joining us on the  coveted 19 special  thank you so much and now it's the part  of the program where we put one of your  questions to our science correspondent  derek williams  i had my second dose of synovac a week  ago  but i also want to get a vaccine  certified by the who and eu  how long should i wait since this  question came in the vaccine  made by cynovac which which is called  the vero  cell vaccine it's received an  emergency use listing from the who  though  it's still under review at the european  medicines agency but i  i still want to look at this topic  because i receive  questions like this one all the time um  that's down to the fact that that in  trials  inactivated virus vaccines made by  chinese manufacturers had lower  efficacy numbers than for example  approved messenger rna vaccines  and there are also now some doubts about  how well they work against the delta  variant of sars  cov2 so so let's look at what the  experts are saying so far  about getting booster shots with the  second type of vaccine  after being vaccinated at least once  with another mixing and matching is  already going on  with different vaccine platforms um big  clinical studies looking at a range of  combinations of different vaccines  are progressing pretty quickly and there  don't seem to be any big  red flags so far um when it comes  to safety many experts believe that  mixing and matching even within the  two-dose regime that most vaccines  require  that that might actually turn out to be  more effective  at preventing covet 19 than  two doses of the same vaccine so  i don't think anyone is expecting that a  third booster shot  with a different vaccine would cause  major problems  in a couple of persian gulf nations for  example they've already begun  giving a third pfizer biotech booster  to some high-risk people who were fully  vaccinated  with a sino-farm vaccine more than six  months ago  the w.h.o however still hasn't made any  concrete recommendations  you

